Level_1_Macro,Microsoft,sfi-nov-2025-progress-report.pdf,"Secure Future Initiative Security above all else November 2025 progress report Summary The Microsoft Secure Future Initiative (SFI) is a continuous commitment to revolutionize the way we design, build, test, and operate our products and services, to achieve the highest security standards. Contents Secure Future Initiative (SFI) Security principles Continuous improvement Paved path Standards Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 Secure by Design Secure by Default Secure Operations Security Culture and Governance Engineering pillars Protect identities and secrets Protect tenants and isolate production systems Protect networks Protect engineering systems Monitor and detect threats Accelerate response and remediation Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary 1 Executive summary 01 Introduction 02 Report background and methodology 04 Progress highlights 05 Customer guidance 08 Culture 09 Governance 12 Security principles 15 Engineering pillars 21 Appendix 34 Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary 2 Introduction “ Security is just part of everything we do. It’s a way of life for inventing technology and using technology.” Charlie Bell EVP, Microsoft Security In our third progress report, we share updates for every area and engineering pillar, introduce mapping to the NIST Cybersecurity Framework1 to help customers understand progress made using a recognized industry framework, and highlight new security capabilities delivered to customers. We also share best practices and implementation guidance, aligned to Zero Trust principles, to help customers reduce their risk. Culture and governance We continue to foster a culture that puts security first by embedding security into our hiring, onboarding, and performance systems. This has reinforced accountability and accelerated cybersecurity talent development at every level, and increased engineering sentiment on security. We have invested in additional governance structures to address cybersecurity risk and compliance, and expanded Deputy Chief Information Security Officer functions to cover additional domains. To address cybersecurity risk on a global scale we implemented the Microsoft European Security Program and participated in multistakeholder diplomacy efforts like the Advancing Regional Cybersecurity Initiative in the Global South. Security principles To help better protect customers, teams across the company delivered 17 innovations aligned with our security principles of Secure by Design, Secure by Default, and Secure Operations, for example: • Microsoft Azure expanded mandatory MFA to all Azure service users and now offers secure-by-default VM connectivity through Azure Bastion Developer, significantly reducing the attack surface by ensuring secure credentials and protected connectivity. • Microsoft 365 introduced dedicated AI and security governance roles to enforce least-privilege for new capabilities, including the AI administrator role and Copilot agent lifecycle controls. • Windows 11 expanded passwordless sign-in support and introduced Quick Machine Recovery capabilities. Microsoft Surface enhanced device security by using firmware and drivers written in memory- safe languages. • Microsoft Purview introduced the ability to centrally manage and proactively monitor AI data security across Copilots, agents, and apps, including those using third-party LLMs. Microsoft Sentinel can now ingest data and correlate signals across domains with new data lake, graph, and Model Context Protocol (MCP) server capabilities. Secure by Design Security comes first when designing any product or service. Secure by Default Security protections are enabled and enforced by default, require no extra effort, and are not optional. Secure Operations Security controls and monitoring will continuously be improved to meet current and future threats. Note 1https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary “ There’s never a day that our mission is accomplished. The threat landscape will continue to evolve.” Joy Chik President, Identity & Network Access and Executive Sponsor, SFI Engineering pillars We continue to make steady progress in every engineering pillar: out of 28 objectives, 5 are nearing completion and 12 have made significant progress. Key achievements include: •␀ 99.6% adoption of phishing-resistant MFA for users and devices. •␀ 44,500 higher-risk users moved to locked-down Azure Virtual Desktops to reduce device compromise risk. •␀ Complete network device inventory and lifecycle management. •␀ 99.5% detection and remediation of live secrets in code. •␀ More than USD $17 million awarded to promote responsible disclosure of vulnerabilities. •␀ 50+ new detections deployed to the Microsoft infrastructure to target high priority tactics, techniques, and procedures – applicable detections will be integrated into Microsoft Defender. This progress drives continuous innovation across our products, services, and security portfolio - Microsoft Entra, Purview, Defender, Sentinel, and Intune. Actionable guidance It also informs the guidance we provide. In this report we highlight 10 patterns and practices customers can follow to reduce their risk in prioritized areas and share additional best practices and guidance for each area and pillar. Key themes include: • Strengthen identity and access controls • Enforce least-privilege, stringently and at scale • Secure multi-tenant environments • Harden software development lifecycle and supply chain • Improve visibility and monitoring • Accelerate detection and response 99.6% adoption of phishing-resistant MFA for users and devices. Continuous improvement With the equivalent of 35,000 engineers working full time to improve security, SFI remains the largest cybersecurity effort in digital history. Looking forward, we will continue to prioritize the highest risks, accelerate the delivery of security innovations to better protect customers, rigorously enforce our Security Durability Model, and use AI to increase engineering efficiency and accelerate threat response. The threat landscape will continue to evolve. Technology will continue to advance. Microsoft will continue to prioritize security above all else. We are grateful for the partnership and look forward to innovating together for a safer future. Introduction continued 35,000 equivalent of full-time Microsoft engineers dedicated to security. 3 Report background and methodology “ From the very beginning Satya has been clear. Our priority is security. And if you have to make a choice between security and something else, you choose security.” Amy Hogan-Burney CVP, Customer Security & Trust Culture, governance, and security principles For each area we provide examples of progress and customer guidance. For principles we include innovation highlights and implementation insights. Engineering pillars In May 2024 we announced the expansion of SFI to include 6 prioritized engineering pillars and 28 aligned objectives. Engineering owners were assigned to each engineering pillar. They established an initial body of work and scope to advance the objective, according to Microsoft priorities. The work was then translated into standards and key results. Since that time, pillar owners have expanded on the initial body of work, adding new scope, standards or key results based on changes in the threat landscape, risk prioritization from the Cybersecurity Governance Council, and learnings from ongoing implementation. In April 2025 we disclosed progress made towards completing each objective. In this report we continue to disclose cumulative progress made towards objectives, introduce mapping to NIST CSF, and provide customer guidance. We calculated progress by recording the completed percentage of standards and key results, those defined initially and any additions. Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary Some objectives focus on a limited scope of products. We do not disclose the full body of work we are undertaking to advance an objective (such as standards and key results) but do share examples of progress made against specific standards and key results. In a few cases progress is not reflected as a percentage completed, instead we show the number of innovations, detections, or no-action cloud CVEs we have created since the previous progress report. Each objective represents a significant body of work to improve security in a specific area and reduce risk for Microsoft and our customers. Some objectives will take several years to complete. Those nearing completion may require ongoing management to continue to maintain risk reduction. Progress made will not be linear. Standards and key results may change over time due to emerging risks or technology. Progress may regress due to an increase in the body of work prioritized to complete the objective or advance rapidly due to innovation. Measuring investments in cybersecurity risk reduction and communicating progress in a durable manner across the magnitude of engineering activities SFI covers is an imperfect science. While this report cannot address every nuance of Microsoft efforts, it is intended to communicate SFI progress in a way that demonstrates our Microsoft security investments and underscores important advancements relevant for the awareness and activities of our customers and the industry. Progress legend Each objective represents a significant body of work, most will take years to complete. Out of 28 objectives, 5 are nearing completion, 12 have made significant progress, and we continue to make progress against the rest. We prioritized the highest risks, most critical assets, and used platform engineering practices to scale the work, drive clear prioritization, and reduce toil. Icon Description Total objectives 0-32% Initial progress 3 33-65% Progress 4 66-94% Significant progress 12 95-99% Nearing completion 5 No percentage 4 Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary 4 Progress highlights This section provides a summary of key progress, with detailed explanations to follow. Culture We continue to foster a culture that puts security first across the organization. • ␀ 95% of employees have already completed the latest security training assigned in July 2025 on Guarding Against AI-Powered Attacks, which remains one of our highest-rated courses. • ␀ Engineering sentiment around security has increased by 9 points since February 2024. • ␀ To reinforce a security-first mindset at work and at home, we developed resources for employees and made them available to customers for the first time to improve security awareness. +9pts Engineering sentiment has increased by 9 points since the initial survey. Governance We continue to scale our governance model to address the evolving threat landscape and increased regulatory complexity. • ␀ Expanded the scope of the Council to include 3 additional Deputy CISO functions: – Supply Chain and Third-Party – Business Functions, Marketing, and Finance – Compliance with EU cybersecurity legislation • ␀ Created the Microsoft European Security Program to deepen partnerships and better inform European governments about the threat landscape. Continued active engagement in the European Union Cyber Resilience Act Expert Group. • ␀ Actively collaborated with industry partners to better align existing and future cybersecurity regulations and build cybersecurity capacity through the Advancing Regional Cybersecurity Initiative in the Global South. +3 Expanded scope to include 3 additional Deputy CISO functions. Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary 5 Security principles Guided by three security principles – Secure by Design, Secure by Default, and Secure Operations – teams across Microsoft continue to deliver innovations to help protect customers and Microsoft. Introduced mandatory secure defaults, expanded hardware-based trust, and updated security benchmarks to help improve cloud security. • • MFA is now mandatory for all Azure users, reducing the risk of password- related attacks. Additionally, Azure Bastion Developer now offers secure- by-default connectivity to virtual machines in 35 regions. • Version 2 of the Microsoft Cloud Security Benchmark (MCSB) provides customers with updated security baseline guidance, which can be implemented using Microsoft Defender for Cloud. • Azure Local increased the number of security default settings by 25% (400 settings). This further simplifies customers’ ability to comply with industry standards and better protects Azure Local nodes from additional threats such as fileless malware. See page 17 Introduced a dedicated AI role, enhanced agent lifecycle governance, and improved data security transparency to give organizations more control and visibility. • ␀ The dedicated AI Administrator role enables more secure, least- privilege management of Copilot and other emerging AI capabilities, eliminating the need for broad administrative access. • Centralized controls enhance agent lifecycle governance by enabling administrators to approve, restrict, and audit agents in Microsoft 3 65 while monitoring access and usage to ensure more secure, compliant operations. • Copilot web search transparency with Microsoft Purview Data Security Posture Management (DSPM) enables prompt-level auditing and investigation of external data sourcing risks. See page 18 Introduced automatic recovery capabilities, expanded passkey and Windows Hello support, and improved memory safety in firmware and drivers. These updates help enforce Zero Trust principles, resulting in improved security and resilience. • ␀ Quick Machine Recovery automatically detects and remediates boot failures via a secure, cloud-connected recovery environment, delivering built-in resilience. • Windows Hello now supports additional passwordless sign-in options, such as Enhanced Sign-in Security fingerprint peripherals, and an API enables passkey managers to leverage Hello for seamless authentication with FIDO2-compliant credentials. • ␀ Surface is improving device security by developing UEFI firmware in memory-safe languages, building drivers in Rust, and working with the ecosystem to open-source these innovations and elevate protection standards across Windows. See page 19 Introduced data security posture management for AI, evolved Sentinel into an AI-first platform with data lake, graph, and MCP capabilities, and delivered new agents to automate high- volume tasks. • ␀ Microsoft Purview DSPM for AI helps secure AI app data and proactively monitor AI use, including Copilots, agents, and other AI apps that use third-party large language modules (LLMs). • Microsoft Sentinel has evolved to be both a SIEM and AI-ready platform, giving defenders a single platform to ingest signals, correlate across domains, and empower AI agents built in Security Copilot, VS C ode using GitHub Copilot, or other developer platforms. • Security Copilot agents introduce autonomous, AI-powered support for high-volume security and IT tasks, integrated across Microsoft Security and partner solutions, available via Microsoft Security Store. See page 20 Progress highlights continued Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary 6 Engineering Out of 28 objectives, 5 are nearing completion, 12 have made significant progress, and we continue to make progress against the rest. As a result of SFI we have improved security in our platform and services, as well as our ability to detect and respond to threats. 1. Protect identities and secrets We have improved identity security for Microsoft services and customers. • ␀ Migrated 95% of Microsoft Entra ID signing VMs to Azure Confidential Compute. • ␀ Moved 94.3% of Microsoft Entra ID security token validation to our standard identity Software Development Kit (SDK). • ␀ Enforced phishing-resistant MFA for 99.6% of Microsoft employees and devices. See page 22 2. Protect tenants and isolate production systems We continue to increase isolation and reduce the risk of lateral movement. • Discontinued the use of Active Directory Federation Services (ADFS) in our productivity environment. • ␀ Migrated 98% of cloud assets managed by Azure Service Manager (ASM) to Azure Resource Manager (ARM). • Decommissioned 560,000 additional unused and aged tenants and 83,000 unused Microsoft Entra ID apps across Microsoft production and productivity environments. See page 24 3. Protect networks Progress made has improved network security and delivered new capabilities for customers. • ␀ Achieved complete network device inventory and mature asset lifecycle management. • ␀ Strengthened security through enhanced isolation and protective controls. • Accelerated adoption of Network Security Perimeter (NSP) with more than 1.1 million resources in learning mode and approximately 500,000 in enforced mode. See page 26 4. Protect engineering systems We have improved the security of systems we use to build, test, and deploy code. • ␀ Code signing is now almost entirely locked to production identities, and secrets detected in code are continuously remediated. • ␀ Near complete software asset inventory enables rapid incident response and universal policy enforcement. • ␀ Expanded secure-by-default pipelines and network isolation, with nearly all production builds and 94% of release pipelines using governed templates. See page 28 5. Monitor and detect threats We are advancing threat hunting, rapid incident response, and unified security controls. •␀ 98% of production infrastructure is centrally tracked, with logs retained for 2 years. •␀ 50+ new detections deployed across Microsoft infrastructure, targeting high-priority tactics, techniques, and procedures (TTPs) – applicable detections will be integrated into Microsoft Defender. • AI integration is accelerating detection lifecycle improvements – from identification to efficacy validation. See page 30 6. Accelerate response and remediation We are increasing the speed of identification, triage, and resolution of vulnerabilities. •␀ AI-based vulnerability triage contributed to a 72% success rate addressing vulnerabilities within our reduced time to mitigate, despite continued increase in volume and scope. •␀ Expedited deployment processes have accelerated vulnerability mitigation. •␀ Microsoft published 1,096 CVEs, including 53 no-action cloud CVEs, and paid out USD $17 million in bounties, demonstrating increased transparency and external engagement. See page 32 Progress highlights continued Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary 7 Customer guidance SFI patterns and practices offer repeatable, proven approaches to solving complex cybersecurity challenges. Each pattern is crafted to address a specific security risk and is grounded in Microsoft’s own experience. Like design patterns in software architecture, these security patterns are modular, extensible, and built for reuse across diverse environments. Each pattern follows a consistent structure – clearly defining the cybersecurity challenge, outlining the problem, describing Microsoft’s solution, offering practical guidance, and clarifying the implications. SFI patterns and practices (Taxonomy) Pattern name: Concise handle to describe a cybersecurity problem Problem: Explain the security challenge and its operational context Solution: Describes how Microsoft addressed the issue internally Guidance: Describes how customers can apply these learning in their own environments Implications: Outcomes and trade-offs of applying this pattern Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary 8 SFI based actionable guidance • Phishing-resistant MFA – Adopt cryptographic, phishing-resistant methods such as passkeys, FIDO2 security keys, and certificate-based authentication across all users and tenants to reduce exposure to credential based attacks. • Eliminate identity lateral movement – Segment access, enforce Conditional Access policies, and restrict risky guest authentication to prevent attackers from pivoting across tenants or roles. • Secure all tenants and their resources – Help eliminate “shadow” tenants. Apply baseline security policies, such as MFA and Conditional Access, to every cloud tenant and retire unused ones, so cyberattackers can’t exploit forgotten, weakly-secured environments. • Remove legacy systems that risk security – Decommission unmanaged tenants and outdated infrastructure to reduce configuration drift and shrink your attack surface. • Standardize secure development pipelines – Implement governed continuous integration and continuous delivery (CI/CD) templates to embed security gates, encourage the generation of Software Bill of Materials (SBOMs), and drive consistent compliance. • Protect the software supply chain – Lock down builds and dependencies. Govern your CI/CD pipelines and package management – use standardized build templates, internal package feeds, and automated scanning to block supply chain cyberattacks before they reach production. • Complete production infrastructure inventory – Ensure real- time visibility into assets and telemetry by consolidating inventories, centralizing monitoring, and removing unused applications. • Centralize access to security logs – Speed up investigations. Standardize and centralize your log collection (with longer retention) so that security teams have unified visibility and can detect and investigate incidents faster – even across complex, multi-cloud environments. • Rapid anomaly detection and response – Leverage AI and user behavior analytics, alongside automated response workflows, to detect malicious activity quickly and reduce dwell time. • Accelerate vulnerability mitigation – Automate detection, triage, and patching workflows. Integrate vulnerability management with orchestration tools to minimize time-to-mitigate and enhance resilience. Customers are encouraged to review the full library of SFI patterns and practices at aka.ms/SFIpnp, as well as the best practices and guidance detailed throughout the report. Culture We continue to foster a culture that puts security first across the organization. Performance and development 10 Engineering sentiment 10 Training and enablement 11 Customer guidance 11 "" We have made security a priority for every single employee at Microsoft, not just the security team.” Vasu Jakkal CVP, Microsoft Security Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 9 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary Culture Security is embedded in how we work, how we lead, and how we measure impact. We continue to foster a culture that puts security first across the organization, driven by direct people activation and HR-led initiatives. The refreshed performance tools, with AI-driven prompts, help employees reflect on how their work supports a more secure Microsoft. Performance and development In the most recent performance cycle, security was fully embedded into our performance management process. Every employee had a Security Core Priority (SCP) as part of their annual priorities and all managers factored employees’ performance on this SCP into reward and recognition decisions. This was the first time that security outcomes explicitly influenced performance ratings for every employee, underscoring that protecting the company is as critical as meeting any business target. Building on this progress, we have updated Performance Check-ins (Connects) to embed security into our new “What you deliver” and “How you work” model. Security is no longer a standalone box – employees must now include it in at least one goal, either as a dedicated security goal or integrated into a business objective. The refreshed performance tools, with AI-driven prompts, help employees reflect on how their work supports a more secure Microsoft. Managers will continue to assess security contributions when evaluating impact and rewards, ensuring a strong link between security outcomes and performance under the new Connects approach. Engineering sentiment Engineering sentiment on security was initially tracked in early 2024, and then again in April 2025, increasing by 9 points since the initial survey. Two specific areas – feeling “equipped to address security challenges” and “encouraged to create secure-by-default products” – rose by 3 percentage points in favorability, a statistically meaningful uptick. These gains illustrate that people feel more empowered and supported to prioritize security, rather than viewing it as an obstacle. A healthy security culture also means people can help improve security and do great work. In the April 2025 survey, about 4 out of 5 engineering employees (79%) now feel they can prioritize security needs while staying productive, up from approximately 75% in the previous survey. In other words, most of our engineers do not see a trade-off between building features and protecting the company – a 4-point improvement in alignment, indicating that the efficiency of security processes has improved. +9pts Engineering sentiment has increased by 9 points since the initial survey. NIST CSF mapping Govern Organizational Context Policy Roles, Responsibilities, and Authorities Identify Improvements Protect Awareness and Training Detect Respond Recover See appendix Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 10 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary Training and enablement We continue to evolve both our training programs and performance practices to ensure security remains a core part of the employee experience. The Security Foundations series is the cornerstone of our learning strategy. 95% of employees have completed the latest training assigned July 2025 on Guarding against AI-powered Attacks, which remains one of our highest-rated courses. Rebuilt annually to reflect the evolving threat landscape, the series now includes dynamic, threat-informed content, real-world scenarios, and updated topics such as identity spoofing and AI-driven attacks. Survey data on the latest course further reinforces the impact of the training*: “I play a critical role in keeping Microsoft secure” – 4.47 “I feel confident in identifying and reporting potential security threats” – 4.43 “My team makes security a top priority in our work” – 4.35 * out of a 5-point scale. In addition, to reinforce a security-first mindset at work and at home, we developed resources for employees and made them available to customers for the first time to improve security awareness. Identity protection guide Critical identity protection practices for reducing your risk of becoming a victim of a cyberattack, at work and at home. See guide Security foundations: Guarding against AI- powered Attacks A reference guide to spotting and protecting yourself against AI-powered attacks. See guide This progress strengthens our security posture in alignment with the NIST CSF categories, specifically supporting the Governance functions of Policy, Organizational Context, and Roles, Responsibilities, and Authorities, the Identify function of Improvement, and the Protect function of Awareness and Training. Culture continued Customer guidance 1. Activate a security-first mindset across people processes Start by embedding security into performance expectations. Every employee should have security as part of their performance objectives and regular check- ins. Encourage employees to tailor these priorities to reflect the specific security responsibilities of their roles. Accountability must be reinforced through systems. Use performance management, talent reviews, and compensation processes to recognize and reward security-focused behaviors and outcomes. Managers play a critical role in reinforcing a culture that puts security first. Equip leaders with the tools and training to coach their teams on security expectations. Regular feedback and development conversations should include security as a standing topic. 2. Build and sustain security talent and capabilities Security-first mindset is only as strong as the people who carry it forward. Partner with HR and business leaders to define what security talent looks like across your organization. Consider establishing core categories such as cybersecurity, compliance, privacy, and operational security, and embed these expectations into job architecture and role descriptions. Accelerate hiring for high-impact security roles. Streamline interview processes and assess for both technical capability and security mindset. Candidates should understand that security is a shared responsibility and a leadership expectation. Integrate security into onboarding. New hires should receive clear guidance on their role in protecting the organization from day one. This early emphasis helps establish security as a cultural norm rather than a procedural requirement. 1 © Copyright Microsoft Corporation. All rights reserved. Classified as Microsoft Public | Security Foundations: Guarding Against AI-powered Attacks This guide summarizes key takeaways and best practices from the internal Microsoft training course, Security Foundations: Guarding Against AI-powered Attacks. Use it for quick, just-in-time reminders to keep yourself, and your organization, secure. Guide contents: • AI-powered attack methods • Spotting deepfakes • Zero Trust security principles • Identity protection practices • Improving your security practices Quick Reference Guide In 2024, threat actors compromised over 3.2 billion credentials, a 33% increase year over year. Flashpoint 2025 Global Threat Intelligence Report Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 11 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary Governance We continue to scale our governance model to address the evolving threat landscape and increased regulatory complexity. Cybersecurity Governance Council 13 Risk frameworks 14 Cybersecurity policy and diplomacy initiatives 14 Customer guidance 14 “ We truly believe that you can’t have a sustainable program if your culture isn’t aligned, and you certainly can’t have a measurable program if your governance isn’t aligned.” Ann Johnson CVP & Deputy CISO, Customer Security Management Office "" Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 12 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary We continue to scale our governance model to address the evolving threat landscape and increased regulatory complexity. The Cybersecurity Governance Council (the Council) anchors our internal efforts while our cybersecurity policy and diplomacy efforts extend outward, advocating for global regulatory alignment. Governance Integrating Deputy CISOs from key product and functional areas into the Council has advanced security as a core part of development. Cybersecurity Governance Council As described in the September 2024 progress report, the Council is responsible for overall cybersecurity risk and compliance. Since the April 2025 progress report, the Council has expanded its scope to include 3 new functions focused on European regulations, internal operations, and engagement with our ecosystem of partners and suppliers: • Supply Chain and Third-party – A dedicated Deputy CISO now leads efforts to secure our third-party ecosystem, spanning hardware, software, and human supply chains. • Business Functions, Marketing, and Finance – The remit of the Deputy CISO for Business Functions has been expanded to include Marketing and Finance. • Europe – A new Deputy CISO for Europe has been appointed to lead compliance efforts across transformative European regulations. Integrating Deputy CISOs from key product and functional areas into the Council has advanced security as a core part of development. That makes it more than just a checkpoint, enabling earlier risk mitigation and improved resilience at scale. Areas covered by Deputy CISO • Artificial Intelligence • Azure • Business Applications • Business Functions, Marketing, and Finance • Commerce • Consumer • Europe • Experiences and Devices • Gaming • Government • Identity • Core Systems, Mergers and Acquisitions • Customer Security Management Office • Microsoft Security • Privacy and Policy • Regulated Industries • Supply Chain and Third-party • Threat Landscape NIST CSF mapping Govern Risk Management Strategy Roles, Responsibilities, and Authorities Identify Protect Detect Respond Recover See appendix Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 13 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary Governance continued Risk frameworks Risk management guides everything that we do. We prioritize the highest risks and consistently review, action, and prioritize those across the Deputy CISOs. As part of our work evaluating risks, we leverage cybersecurity industry frameworks such as NIST CSF. Mapping SFI to NIST CSF enables clear communication to enhance comprehensive security and compliance. Microsoft extends these efforts by aligning engineering practices with global standards, including ISO/IEC 27001 and industry frameworks such as PCI DSS and HIPAA, ensuring products meet rigorous requirements. SFI objectives, like identity protection and risk management, also align to objectives detailed in other widely adopted cybersecurity standards, such as NIST SP 800-53, embedding continual improvement and risk mitigation into the engineering lifecycle. Automated processes validate control effectiveness and centralize compliance reporting for executive visibility. The Microsoft compliance portfolio covers a broad range of certifications and attestations across global, industry-specific, and regional standards. This progress strengthens our security posture in alignment with the NIST CSF categories, specifically supporting the Governance functions of Policy, Organizational Context, and Roles, Responsibilities, and Authorities, the Identify function of Improvement, and the Protect function of Awareness and Training. Cybersecurity policy and diplomacy initiatives Our cybersecurity policy and diplomacy efforts extend our SFI work to the broader ecosystem by advocating for governments, regulators, and civil society to make security a top public policy priority. From advancing public policy on AI security to engaging policymakers worldwide to accelerate the transition to quantum-safe cryptography, Microsoft is embedding cybersecurity into both technology and governance. In Europe, we are rolling out the Microsoft European Security Program to deepen partnerships and better inform governments about the threat landscape while continuing active engagement in the European Union Cyber Resilience Act Expert Group (CRA). Globally, we are actively collaborating with industry partners to better align existing and future cybersecurity regulations and build capacity through the Advancing Regional Cybersecurity Initiative in the Global South – driving scalable, regionally grounded approaches to cyber resilience. For more than a decade, Microsoft has advanced responsible behavior in cyberspace – by both governments and industry – through sustained global and bilateral engagement, earning recognition as a trusted thought leader and influential force in multistakeholder diplomacy. You can read more on our work on cybersecurity policy and diplomacy at: https://aka.ms/PolicyandDiplomacy Microsoft is embedding cybersecurity into both technology and governance. Customer guidance • Conduct NIST CSF Security Assessments: At least once a year, conduct assessment exercise based on the guidance provided in the NIST CSF, for the most critical business services. Microsoft Learn • Build cross-functional oversight into your operating model: Establish cross-functional governance structures that include engineering, compliance, and business leadership – ensuring security decisions are owned, reviewed, and escalated with clarity. • Assign domain-specific accountability: Designate governance leads for high-risk areas like supply chain, regional compliance, product areas, and internal operations to tailor oversight to the complexity of each risk surface. • Use regulatory frameworks as resilience drivers: Treat emerging regulations (e.g., DORA, NIS 2, CRA) as opportunities to strengthen enterprise-wide security posture. • Treat cybersecurity policy as a strategic enabler of operational resilience and cross-border trust: Proactively engage in multistakeholder forums, align with international cybersecurity norms, and advocate for regulatory harmonization across jurisdictions. Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 14 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary Security principles To reinforce our commitment to embedding security at every level, we have adopted a company-wide approach centered on three foundational principles: Secure by Design, Secure by Default, and Secure Operations. Security Principles Introduction 16 Microsoft Azure 17 Microsoft 365 18 Windows and Surface 19 Microsoft Security 20 “ One way that SFI is helping security innovations reach customers is to ensure that we’re Secure by Design, Secure by Default, and have Secure Operations, because the more secure we are, the more secure customers will be.” Mark Russinovich CTO, Deputy CISO and Technical Fellow, Microsoft Azure "" Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 15 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary Security principles introduction In this section we highlight 17 new security capabilities in Microsoft Azure, Microsoft 365, Windows, Surface, and the Microsoft Security portfolio. We also share actionable guidance for customers and share insights into how we use these capabilities to better secure Microsoft. The Security Principles strengthen our security posture in alignment with the NIST CSF categories, specifically supporting the Identify function of Improvement and the Protect function of Platform Security. 17 new security capabilities in Microsoft Azure, Microsoft 365, Windows, Surface, and the Microsoft Security portfolio. Secure by Design Security comes first when designing any product or service. Secure by Default Security protections are enabled and enforced by default, require no extra effort, and are not optional. Secure Operations Security controls and monitoring will continuously be improved to meet current and future threats. NIST CSF mapping Govern Identify Improvement Protect Platform Security Detect Respond Recover See appendix Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 16 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary Microsoft Azure Mandatory MFA enforced for all users, Azure Bastion Developer secure-by-default connectivity, integrated hardware-based trust, and updated security benchmarks help improve cloud security. These capabilities reduce risk, harden infrastructure, and simplify compliance, to help organizations build resilient, trustworthy systems. Innovation highlights • Mandatory MFA: Azure has entered phase 2 of mandatory MFA enforcement (starting October 1, 2025), requiring strong authentication for all Azure service users including Command Line Interface (CLI), PowerShell, and mobile apps. This mandatory enforcement improves identity security by neutralizing stolen credentials at scale. • Azure Bastion Developer: Now available across 35 Azure regions at no additional cost, Azure Bastion Developer provides secure web-based VM access without jump boxes or inbound ports – significantly reducing the attack surface while streamlining administration. • Integrated HSM chip: New Azure server hardware features Microsoft-designed HSM chips that meet FIPS 140-3 Level 3 standards. Embedded directly on servers, these tamper-resistant chips keep encryption keys within secure hardware boundaries – eliminating latency and exposure risks. The HSM operates transparently by default for supported services like Azure Key Vault and Storage encryption. • Microsoft Cloud Security Benchmark v2: Microsoft has released MCSB v2 (currently in preview), offering comprehensive Azure security best practices aligned with industry frameworks. The updated benchmark spans identity, networking, compute, data, and management layers, and includes new guidance for AI and confidential computing workloads, with recommendations that can be enforced and monitored through Azure Policy. • Azure Local improvements: Azure Local is a distributed infrastructure solution, enabled by Azure Arc, that provides secure-by-default posture at deployment. We have increased the number of security settings enabled by default by 25% (400 settings) futher simplifying the customers journey to be compliant with industry standards such as CIS and STIG. These additional security defaults help protect Azure Local nodes from additional threats such as fileless malware. Implementation insights • Mandatory MFA: Mandatory MFA has been enforced across all services, emphasizing phishing-resistant methods such as FIDO2 keys and certificates. Microsoft internal telemetry shows this implementation has blocked more than 99% of account compromise attempts and significantly reduced password- related attacks. • Integrated HSM: Azure Integrated HSM will be installed in every new server in Microsoft Azure datacenters (starting with AMD D and E Series v7 Preview). Customer guidance • MFA as default: Require MFA for all users across your environment to strengthen account security and reduce the risk of compromise. Microsoft Learn • Hardware-enforced trust: Leverage Azure Key Vault or Azure Managed HSM to securely store, manage, and rotate your encryption keys. Microsoft Learn • Azure Policy: Use Azure Policy to enforce secure configuration baselines based on the MCSB v2, ensuring your environment aligns with industry- recognized security standards. Microsoft Learn 99% of account takeover attacks blocked by enforcing MFA across Azure. Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 17 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary 18 Microsoft 365 A dedicated AI role, enhanced agent lifecycle governance, and improved data security transparency to give organizations more control and visibility. These capabilities provide comprehensive auditability of AI and user activities, improve AI agent controls, and embed stronger protections by default. Innovation highlights • AI administrator role: Dedicated AI Admin role improves secure Copilot management without global access, enforcing least-privilege principles and isolating AI controls from tenant-wide settings. • Microsoft 365 agent lifecycle controls: Microsoft 365 now provides centralized controls to govern use of agents. Admins can centrally approve, restrict, and audit agents in Microsoft 365 – defining access by user/ group and monitoring commands and data usage to prevent unauthorized activity. • Microsoft Purview DSPM insights for Microsoft 365 Copilot Web Activity: To address AI data sourcing, Microsoft 365 now logs Copilot web search activity via Microsoft Purview DSPM, enabling prompt- level auditing and investigation of external data sourcing risks. Implementation insights • Automated data protection: Microsoft Purview adaptive labeling now classifies and secures more than 50 million items monthly, enhancing compliance at scale. Customer guidance • Use the AI administrator role: Delegate Copilot tasks to specialized teams using Microsoft 365 AI administrator role – enforcing least-privilege, segregation of duties, and secure operational boundaries. Microsoft Learn • Assign permissions for agents in Microsoft 365: Grant agents either Application or Delegated permissions, allowing appropriate actions based on the assigned access scope. Microsoft Learn • Enable Microsoft Purview DSPM for AI: When deploying Microsoft 365 Copilot to monitor and protect sensitive data, enable Microsoft Purview DSPM to monitor Copilot data use, enforce sensitivity labels, and detect misuse – ensuring more secure, compliant AI adoption. Microsoft Learn 50M+ Microsoft Purview now automatically classifies more than 50 million items per month and enforces default and persistent labeling for sensitive data. Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary 19 Windows and Surface Windows now offers cloud-based machine recovery, and passwordless sign-in with passkeys. Additional improvements include better support for drivers written in Rust (a memory safe language) – as approximately 50% of security vulnerabilities, that Microsoft fixes and assigns a Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVE), are due to memory vulnerabilities. These capabilities strengthen security and reliability, while delivering an improved user experience. Innovation highlights • Quick Machine Recovery: Windows 11 can automatically detect and remediate boot failures via a secure, cloud-connected recovery environment. It enables remote fixes, reduces downtime, and protects against boot-time attacks. Recovery operations are performed over an encrypted channel, ensuring integrity and confidentiality during the process. • Passkeys and passwordless: Windows 11, with the integration of Windows Hello and passkeys, offers a more secure and phishing-resistant passwordless sign-in experience using FIDO2-compliant credentials. Windows Hello now supports external Enhanced Sign-in Security fingerprint peripherals. A new API allows passkey managers to plug into the Windows 11 platform and use Windows Hello. This means users can seamlessly use the same passkey on Windows 11 that they created on their mobile devices, ensuring both ease of use and robust security. • Trusted system software: Surface is advancing Windows security through the Open Device Partnership, an open-source firmware initiative that delivers UEFI firmware using a memory-safe language and embedded controller implementations. By replacing legacy firmware with a transparent, secure, and reusable platform, Surface is supporting and enabling new standards for device trust and helping to shape the future of secure firmware across the industry. • Memory-safe Surface drivers: Surface is enhancing device security by rewriting Windows drivers in Rust via a Microsoft led, open-source project. Rust provides a memory-safe approach by design to help prevent common memory flaws like buffer overruns and use- after-free errors, enabling OEMs to build safer drivers across the Windows ecosystem. In 2022 and 2023, approximately half of Microsoft patches addressed memory safety CVEs which may be reduced with greater use of memory-safe languages. • Hotpatch improvements: Hotpatch allows you to update your Windows client without requiring a restart after installation – minimizing downtime and keeping users productive. Hotpatch now supports ARM64 devices in addition to existing support for x64 Intel and AMD architectures. Additionally, hotpatch is now enabled by default when creating a new Quality Update Policy in Autopatch, making it easier than ever to maintain security and compliance with less disruption. Implementation insights • Hotpatch improvements: With hotpatch, we see 81% of enrolled Windows devices become compliant within 24 hours of Patch Tuesday, 90% within 5 days, and 95% in less than 3 weeks. Customer guidance • Quick Machine Recovery: Enable Quick Machine Recovery on Windows 11 to automatically remediate boot failures and protect against boot-time attacks. Microsoft Learn • Passkeys and passwordless: Adopt passwordless sign-ins with Windows 11 Passkeys and FIDO2 credentials, integrating with password managers like 1Password and Bitwarden. Microsoft Learn • Use hotpatch for seamless updates: Protect your organization with the latest security updates for Windows devices while minimizing user disruption. Hotpatch updates are regular Monthly B release security updates that install and take effect without requiring the user to restart the device, ensuring faster compliance and less downtime. Microsoft Learn 81% of enrolled Windows devices become compliant within 24 hours of Patch Tuesday. Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary 20 Microsoft Security Expanded AI data risk protections, introduced new agentic security platform capabilities including data lake, graph, and MCP server. Delivered new Copilot agents to automate repetitive IT and security r elated tasks. Innovation highlights • Microsoft Purview: DSPM enables monitoring of cross-cloud data and user risk through dynamic reports and trend analysis. Additionally, DSPM for AI provides a central management location to quickly secure data for AI apps and proactively monitor AI use, including Copilots, agents, and other AI apps that use third-party large language models (LLMs). • Microsoft Sentinel platform: Microsoft Sentinel data lake enables a tenant-wide repository for collecting, storing, and managing large volumes of security-related data from various sources, whereas Microsoft Sentinel graph empowers context and data visualization across various security experiences. With Sentinel Model Context Protocol (MCP), customers can now interactively query security data in natural language and build effective security agents that can perform complex automation. • Microsoft Security Copilot: There are more than 35 Microsoft and partner-developed agents across Microsoft Entra, Microsoft Purview, Defender, and Intune to automate repetitive tasks and reduce manual workloads. For example, the Phishing Triage agent reduces alert fatigue and boosts threat response – analysts identified 6.5x more malicious emails per minute with up to 77% higher accuracy, increasing efficiency by 550%, all without blind approvals. Partner- developed agents are now available via the new Microsoft Security Store. Implementation insights • Microsoft Entra: Microsoft Entra ID Governance enables self-service and standardized least-privilege policy-based access across productivity and engineering environments. As an example, Microsoft Entra ID entitlement management and corresponding access packages are being used to govern access across Microsoft engineering teams. This provides a more secure method to gain access to source code and deployment pipelines to carry out provision, approve, revoke, and de-provision activities. We also define just- in-time access policies across all roles including admin, service engineers, and developers. • Microsoft Purview: The deployment at Microsoft now automatically classifies more than 50 million items per month and enforces default and persistent labeling for sensitive data. Adaptive Protection with data loss prevention policies block risky sharing activities based on user behavior across USB drives, web, email, and Teams, providing proactive and consistent protection across cloud and on-premises environments. Customer guidance • Consolidate security data: Deploy Microsoft Sentinel data lake to ingest, store, and analyze large volumes of diverse security data at scale. Onboard to Microsoft Sentinel data lake and Microsoft Sentinel graph. Microsoft Learn • Leverage agentic security: Enable Security Copilot agents for analysts so they can benefit from its AI capabilities covering incident triage, reporting, and more. Discover and leverage Microsoft Security Copilot agents. Microsoft Learn • Mitigate the risks associated with AI and Data: Deploy Microsoft Purview DSPM for AI to mitigate and manage the risks associated with AI usage by implementing corresponding protection and governance controls. Microsoft Learn 550% increased efficiency with Phishing Triage agent. “ Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 Protect identities and secrets 22 Protect tenants and isolate production systems 24 Protect networks 26 Protect engineering systems 28 Monitor and detect threats 30 Accelerate response and remediation 32 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary 21 Engineering pillars The six SFI pillars include goals and actions that define our approach to security. “ As a result of SFI we have improved security in our platform and services, as well as our ability to detect and respond to threats.” Joy Chik President, Identity & Network Access and Executive Sponsor, SFI Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary 22 1. Protect identities and secrets We are committed to reducing risk, enhancing operational maturity, and proactively preparing for emerging threats to identity infrastructure by prioritizing secure authentication and robust key management. We are near complete for Objective 3. We have enforced phishing-resistant MFA for 99.6% of users and devices across Microsoft tenants, significantly strengthening the security of our productivity environment. We have made significant progress in Objectives 1, 2, and 4. We migrated 95% of Microsoft Entra ID signing VMs to Azure Confidential Compute to further protect identity cryptographic keys. We also moved more than 94% of Microsoft Entra ID tokens to be validated by our standard identity SDKs and will be making these implementations available to customers. Additionally, we expanded our Safe Secrets Standard to include Redis Cache, with Azure Policy auditing safe secrets adherence at scale. We have also made progress in Objectives 5-7. We expanded asynchronous, stateful token validation to detect forged tokens for Exchange Online, SharePoint Online, Azure Key Vault, Azure Resource Manager, and Microsoft Graph, and broadened security token revocation to Azure Key Vault, Privileged Access Management, and Teams. We also completed our initial milestones for partitioning identity signing keys in Public cloud. In August, we provided our roadmap for the Quantum Safe Program (QSP). We are building on Windows and SymCrypt work to enable cryptographic agility across identity platforms and certificate services to better protect identity and trust chains in the post-quantum era. This progress directly strengthens our security posture in alignment with the NIST CSF categories, specifically supporting the Protect functions of Identity Management, Authentication, and Access Control, along with Data Security and Platform Security, and the Detect function of Anamoalies and Events. Customers should implement phishing-resistant MFA and enforce with Conditional Access policies; leverage Azure Managed Identities and related Azure Policies for secure credential management and adopt standard SDKs for identity verification across workloads. These technologies support the Zero Trust pillars of Identity, Apps, and Infrastructure, ensuring that authentication is continuously validated based on context. Objectives Progress 1. Protect cryptographic signing keys 2. Adopt standard SDKs for identity 3. Phishing-resistant MFA 4. Safe Secrets Standard 5. Stateful validation for identity tokens 6. Fine-grained key partitioning 7. Quantum-safe PKI systems Customer guidance • Phishing-resistant MFA Enforce phishing-resistant MFA using FIDO2, passkeys, and Conditional Access policies Microsoft Learn • Azure Managed Identities Adopt Azure Managed Identities for secure, automated credential management Microsoft Learn • Adopt standard SDKs for identity Implement MSAL and Microsoft Entra SDK for Cloud Services, which will soon be made available to customers Microsoft Learn NIST CSF mapping Govern Identify Protect Identity Management, Authentication, and Access Control Data Security Platform Security Detect Anomalies and events Respond Recover See appendix Protect identities and secrets continued Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary 23 Objectives Narrative CSF Sub-categories 1. Protect cryptographic signing keys We have moved 95% of Microsoft Entra ID signing VMs to Azure Confidential Compute, which is now the largest confidential compute workload in Azure, surpassing Microsoft Account which we announced in April. PR.AA-04 PR.PS-01 2. Adopt standard SDKs for identity We expanded our measurement of standard SDK adoption to include Microsoft Account and internal platform identity systems and are now validating more than 94% of all Microsoft Entra ID security tokens in Microsoft with our standard SDKs. We have already released Microsoft Authentication Library (MSAL) as a common implementation and will release our Microsoft Entra SDK for Cloud Service at Ignite, starting with scenarios most relevant for AI workloads so customers can use this to secure their agentic systems. We have also made substantial progress in removing legacy authentication protocols – specifically multiple protocols removed across Microsoft 365 workloads and actively being phased out in Microsoft Entra ID. These efforts strengthen our identity platform and improve resilience against evolving threats. PR.AA-01 PR.AA-02 PR.AA-03 3. Phishing- resistant MFA We have enforced phishing-resistant MFA on 99.6% of users and devices across all Microsoft tenants. Notably, we have achieved 100% compliance on iOS and 99.97% on Android. PR.AA-01 PR.AA-03 4. Safe Secrets Standard We have expanded our Safe Secrets Standard to include Redis Cache and will now begin adoption, further strengthening our Safe Secrets Standard across Microsoft Entra ID apps and Azure resources. We use Azure Policy to monitor and audit adherence to this standard for more than 44 million resources, which represents the second largest Azure Policy deployment in Azure. These improvements help ensure consistent credential hygiene and reduce the risk of secret mishandling. Our coverage continues to grow across Microsoft environments, reinforcing secure service-to-service authentication and aligning with our broader data protection strategy. PR.AA-04 PR.DS-01 PR.PS-01 Objectives Narrative CSF Sub-categories 5. Stateful validation for identity tokens We have expanded asynchronous token validation to include forged token detection for the top 5 Microsoft applications – Microsoft Exchange Online, Microsoft SharePoint Online, Azure Key Vault, Azure Resource Manager, and Microsoft Graph. Additionally, more services now support rapid access revocation if app credentials or tokens are compromised, minimizing exposure. This includes Azure Key Vault, Microsoft Entra ID Privileged Identity Management, and Teams. These advancements strengthen our ability to detect and respond to token forgery and credential misuse in real-time. PR.AA-03 DE.AE-02 DE.AE-07 6. Fine-grained key partitioning We have completed our first milestone for partitioning signing within the Microsoft Entra ID infrastructure. This initial milestone reduces blast radius so that compromised keys cannot enable traversal into higher-privilege management layers within the infrastructure. This work complements our broader signing key protection efforts and lays the foundation for future compartmentalization across additional infrastructure layers. PR.AA-04 PR.AA-05 7. Quantum-safe PKI systems Aligned with the Microsoft QSP, we are advancing from algorithm integration to the design and implementation of quantum-resistant public key infrastructure (PKI) systems. Building on the integration of post-quantum cryptography (PQC) algorithms into SymCrypt and core OS components, we are now enabling cryptographic agility across certificate services, identity platforms, and the ecosystem of services atop the platform. Key milestones include preparing Active Directory Certificate Services (ADCS) for PQC support, establishing a root Certificate Authority capable of issuing post-quantum certificates, and enhancing token validation and isolation for critical services such as Azure Key Vault and Microsoft Entra. These efforts lay the foundation for a secure, interoperable PKI ecosystem that will protect identities and trust chains in a post-quantum era, starting with general availability of PQC algorithms in Windows Server 2025, Windows 11 client, and .NET 10, coming in 2025, followed by ADCS in early 2026. PR.AA-04 See appendix Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary 24 2. Protect tenants and isolate production systems We focus on reducing the blast radius in the Microsoft cloud by eliminating legacy systems, securing tenant boundaries, enforcing least-privilege, and hardening defenses against lateral movement. We have made significant progress against Objectives 1, and 3-6. We have fully retired the use of Active Directory Federation Services (ADFS) in our productivity environment, accelerated the migration of cloud asset management from ASM to ARM, achieving 98%, and we have retired an additional 560,000 unused and aged tenants that no longer met security or business requirements. We reconfigured tenancy for more than 25,000 multi-tenant Microsoft Entra ID apps and removed 98% of cross boundary secrets, significantly reducing the risk that tokens or credentials can be reused to gain access across environments. We identified and eliminated excessive permission, which reduced more than 3,000 potential paths for privilege escalation. As of September 2025, we have moved more than 44,500 higher-risk users to a locked d own Azure Virtual Desktop (AVD) infrastructure. Access to tenants is restricted to trusted endpoints, cutting off a major avenue for device-based compromise. We have made progress against Objective 2. We continued to expand tenant discovery and governance, ensuring visibility into production, productivity, and ephemeral tenants. By applying security baselines across these environments and enforcing lifecycle governance, we reduced shadow tenant sprawl and increased our ability to enforce consistent protections across the fleet. This progress directly strengthens our security posture in alignment with the NIST CSF categories, specifically supporting the Identify function of Asset Management, the Detect function of Continuous Monitoring, and contributing to the Protect functions areas of Platform Security, Infrastructure Resilience, and Identity Management, Authentication, and Access Control. To align with Zero Trust principles, organizations should eliminate legacy systems that lack modern security controls, establish strong security baselines, and automate identity and access lifecycle management to reduce human error and policy drift. Migrating higher-risk users to hardened, policy-enforced endpoints helps ensure that access is continuously verified and risk-adaptive controls are applied. Objectives Progress 1. Remove legacy systems that risk security 2. Secure all tenants and their resources 3. Higher security for Microsoft Entra ID apps 4. Eliminate identity lateral movement 5. Continuous least-privilege enforcement 6. Secure devices used for access Customer guidance • Decommission legacy tenants and resources Retire and decommission applications and tenants using structured lifecycle management and security controls Microsoft Learn • Eliminate identity lateral movement Enforce tenant isolation, Conditional Access, and least-privilege to prevent lateral movement Microsoft Learn • Secure devices used for access Enforce device compliance and secure remote access with Azure Virtual Desktop Microsoft Learn NIST CSF mapping Govern Identify Asset Management Protect Identity Management, Authentication, and Access Control Infrastructure Resilience Platform Security Detect Continuous Monitoring Respond Recover See appendix Protect tenants and isolate production systems continued Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary 25 Objectives Narrative CSF Sub-categories 1. Remove legacy systems that risk security We are reducing our legacy footprint across identity provisioning, resource management, and unused tenants. A key area of focus is the modernization of identity provisioning by shifting the Source of Authority (SOA) from on-premises Active Directory to cloud-native platforms, like Microsoft Entra ID. This transition strengthens security with advanced controls like modern MFA, Conditional Access, and real-time threat detection while eliminating vulnerabilities inherent in hybrid identity models and aligning to Zero Trust principles. We have retired the use of ADFS in our productivity environment, advanced the migration of ASM to ARM (from 88% in April to more than 98% today), and eliminated an additional 560,000 unused and aged tenants (more than 6.9 million in total) that no longer met security or business requirements. ID.AM-02 ID.AM-08 PR.PS-01 2. Secure all tenants and their resources We have strengthened tenant segmentation by removing employee created test tenants, or unused tenants, with a focus on purpose-built tenants, which has materially reduced unmanaged lateral movement paths. This promotes consistent policy enforcement and reduces lateral movement risk, which aligns with the principle of Secure by Design. To support this model, and enable employee productivity, we created an auxiliary tenant fleet – a class of purpose-built, non-production tenants used for testing, development, demos, and partner collaboration. These auxiliary tenants are governed with strict access controls, scoped privileges, and lifecycle policies to help ensure they remain isolated, and compliant. ID.AM-02 PR.AA-05 PR.IR-01 PR.PS-01 3. High security for Microsoft Entra ID apps Applications remain a major vector for lateral movement if misconfigured or vulnerable. We have transitioned to automated lifetime management for Microsoft Entra ID apps in our productivity environment, ensuring continuous security posture enforcement. We have eliminated 1.2 million unused apps (an increase of 83,000 since April) and reconfigured 25,199 multi-tenant apps to restrict authentication from intended tenants and reduce lateral movement risk across environments. Network restrictions have been added to 5.9 million managed identities (up from 4.4 million since April), and a revised emergency response system now supports thousands of apps across all environments. ID.AM-08 PR.AA-01 PR.AA-05 PR.IR-01 Objectives Narrative CSF Sub-categories 4. Eliminate identity lateral movement When an organization has multiple tenants, lateral movement across tenant boundaries is one of the most exploited attack paths. We improved cross-boundary secret isolation from 94% in April to 98% today, closing thousands of credential paths that allowed tokens to be created across environments. Each secret removed directly reduces the risk of adversaries pivoting across tenants, which is why achieving full remediation remains an important objective. We are also stamping certificates with tenant and cloud metadata for improved traceability. These efforts help us identify cross-boundary traversal risk so we can plan effective measures to enforce secure identity segmentation. PR.AA-04 PR.IR-01 DE.CM-01 5. Continuous least-privilege enforcement We are advancing least-privilege enforcement across Microsoft services, reducing persistent and excessive access to ensure a secure-by-default posture that minimizes risk from over-permissioned identities. Our high- privilege application reduction program is now 78% complete, significantly lowering the attack surface by migrating thousands of applications to least-privilege models. We are rolling out additional enhancements to our authorization model to further strengthen Microsoft services secure-by-default posture, as well as introducing a new authentication flow designed to drive further reductions in high-privilege applications. PR.AA-05 6. Secure devices used for access All production access across Microsoft tenants requires locked-down endpoints. We have deployed 112,000 production ready locked-down devices (an increase of 14,000 since April), blocking unmanaged endpoints from reaching production systems. For productivity environments, as of September 2025, we have moved 44,516 higher-risk users to AVDs (up from 28,000 in April). This eliminates endpoints without full lockdown as an entry vector into Microsoft tenants. Our secure device posture ensures privileged operations can only be performed from compliant endpoints and non-compliant devices are automatically blocked, reducing unmanaged access attempts. ID.AM-01 ID.AM-02 PR.AA-01 PR.AA-06 PR.IR-01 See appendix Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary 26 3. Protect networks Our efforts are focused on the enhancement of network security and operational maturity by implementing robust asset management, network isolation, and secure-by-default capabilities to safeguard resources and minimize vulnerabilities. We continue to drive near complete status for Objective 1. We have made substantial progress advancing operational maturity across asset management. Network device inventory and lifecycle management have now achieved complete status for our production network, with all production environment network devices meeting our stringent security standards. We have made significant progress for Objectives 2 and 3. Enhanced network isolation has been implemented throughout production environments, with enforced controls such as virtual private networks (VPNs), firewalls, and restricted access, as well as significantly reducing the number of cross-network peering points. Adoption of the Network Security Perimeter (NSP) feature has accelerated, with more than 1.1 million resources in learning mode, approximately 500,000 in enforced mode, and public access has been disabled for hundreds of thousands of additional resources. Azure Policy has played a critical role in ensuring that the principle of Secure by Default remains intact over time, preventing regression, which further strengthens our security posture. The general availability of NSP empowers customers to define secure boundaries for their PaaS storage resources and Bastion Developer now provides private connectivity to virtual machines, eliminating the need for public IP addresses and reducing exposure to external threats. These ongoing measures have minimized entry points, limited lateral movement, and closed detection gaps. Continued investments in micro-segmentation of services, perimeter controls, and expanded telemetry coverage are reinforcing network security for both Microsoft and its customers. This progress directly strengthens our security posture in alignment with the NIST CSF categories, specifically supporting the Identify function for Asset Management, and the Protect functions of Data Security, Platform Security, Infrastructure Resilience, and Identity Management, Authentication, and Access Control. To align with Zero Trust principles, organizations should adopt secure-by-default network configurations, enforce network segmentation and isolation, and implement asset-centric perimeter controls that continuously validate trust across all endpoints and workloads – including Azure NSP. These practices support the Zero Trust pillars of Network, Devices, and Infrastructure, and reflect the principles of assume breach, verify explicitly, and use least-privilege. Objectives Progress 1. Track and maintain inventory and compliance of network devices 2. Apply network isolation to limit blast radius and lateral movement 3. Empowering customers with secure- by-default network policies 3 new capabilities Customer guidance • Apply micro-segmentation and network isolation Implement segmentation strategies and Zero Trust principles to limit blast radius and lateral movement Microsoft Learn • Enforce centralized ACLs and perimeter controls Configure and manage ACLs for network security Microsoft Learn • Establish a network security perimeter for cloud resources Implement Azure Network Security Perimeter to create logical boundaries and restrict public access Microsoft Learn NIST CSF mapping Govern Identify Asset Management Protect Identity Management, Authentication, and Access Control Infrastructure Resilience Platform Security Detect Respond Recover See appendix Protect networks continued Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary Objectives Narrative CSF Sub-categories 27 Objectives Narrative CSF Sub-categories 1. Inventory and security standards We have achieved substantial operational maturity securing Microsoft production networks. Our improved telemetry provides increased asset visibility, enabling complete inventory of all production network devices to be tracked in a central inventory with lifecycle metadata. All network devices in our production networks are configured with centralized authentication, audit trail, and ACLs for IPv4/IPv6 to restrict lateral movement. We also implemented durability controls to maintain this posture (Start Green) and to maintain secure-by-default provisioning (Stay Green). Ongoing efforts focus on resolving detection anomalies and preventing regression across environments. ID.AM-01 ID.AM-03 PR.AA-05 PR.PS-01 PR.PS-04 2. Network isolation We are continuing to expand network isolation across Microsoft production environments using micro-segmentation and perimeter controls. NSP adoption has grown to 1.1 million resources in learning mode and approximately 500,000 in enforced mode. Public access has been disabled for hundreds of thousands of resources. Tagged first-party IPs increased to 21%, supporting fine-grained segmentation. Azure Virtual Network Manager now enforces centralized ACLs across more than 6,000 services. These controls reduce lateral movement risk and strengthen defense layers. PR.IR-01 PR.PS-01 PR.PS-04 3. Secure customer cloud networks The NSP feature has reached general availability, allowing customers to define logical isolation boundaries around PaaS resources. Azure Bastion Developer now supports private connectivity to Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS) clusters, enabling more secure access to the AKS API server without public exposure or complex VPN setups. Private Application Gateway v2 is also generally available, offering enhanced control of public-facing endpoints. These capabilities make secure-by-default deployment easier and strengthen customer network isolation and perimeter defense. PR.IR-01 PR.PS-01 See appendix 4. Protect engineering systems We are focused on enhancing security by operationalizing asset inventory, implementing Zero Trust principles, securing pipelines, and strengthening network isolation to reduce risk and help ensure resilient engineering operations. We continue to drive near completion of Objectives 2 and 3. Code signing access is now almost entirely locked to production identities, ensuring that only authorized personnel can perform critical operations and live secret detection and remediation operates at 99.5%, helping to prevent credential leaks in real- time. Zero Trust code-access has been further operationalized through the adoption of just-in-time admin elevation, short- lived credentials, and proof-of-presence checks, which are now standard f or nearly all production branches. We have made significant progress for all other Objectives. We have made substantial progress in operationalizing security and reducing risk across our engineering systems, reflecting the effectiveness of our current initiatives. Notably, asset inventory coverage is now near complete, with Start Green and Stay Green ensuring that 80% of assets are inventoried at creation and the remainder within 24 hours. This rapid inventory process enables swift incident response and supports universal policy rollout. With the success of these controls, we leveraged advanced analytics to identify a broader code base to expand protections. Secure-by-default pipelines have also seen significant expansion, with nearly all production build and 94% of release pipelines utilizing centrally governed templates. Additionally, tenant isolation for build and test environments has reached 89%, further strengthening our security controls. The adoption of internal package feeds and automated open- source vulnerability remediation through AI agents continues to grow, with pull-request time blocks for packages with vulnerabilities. Component Governance and Centralized Feed Service are now broadly adopted across teams. This progress directly strengthens our security posture in alignment with the NIST CSF categories, specifically supporting the Governance function of Cybersecurity Supply Chain Risk Management, the Identify functions of Asset Management and Risk Management, and the Protect functions of Data Security, Platform Security, and Identity Management, Authentication, and Access Control. To reduce vulnerabilities across the software development lifecycle, customers should apply Zero Trust principles to code access, ensuring that source-code repositories enforce least- privilege, just-in-time elevation, and proof-of-presence for all production branches – this minimizes lateral movement and protects high-value assets from unauthorized changes. Customers should also use internal package feeds to reduce exposure to unverified third-party components and enable software composition analysis (SCA) tools to continuously monitor dependencies for known vulnerabilities. Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 Objectives Progress 1. Complete software asset inventory 2. Zero Trust for source code access 3. Secure code deployment 4. Standardize secure development pipelines 5. Protect the software supply chain Customer guidance • ␀ Zero Trust access for code Enforce Conditional Access and MFA for code access Microsoft Learn • ␀ Pipeline governance Adopt YAML templates with SDL enforcement Microsoft Learn • ␀ Supply chain protection Use internal feeds and enable software composition analysis tools Microsoft Learn GitHub Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary 28 NIST CSF mapping Govern Cybersecurity Supply Chain Risk Management Identify Asset Management Risk Assessment Protect Identity Management, Authentication, and Access Control Data Security Platform Security Detect Respond Recover See appendix Protect engineering systems continued Objectives Narrative CSF Sub-categories 1. Complete software asset inventory Inventory spans all Azure DevOps repositories and pipelines with full metadata. Our StartRight tooling ensures 80% of assets are inventoried at creation; our StayRight process captures the rest within 24 hours. This enables rapid incident response and universal policy enforcement. We increased our inventory scope with finer-grained ownership for better response. ID.AM-02 2. Zero Trust for source code access Standing access for admin roles has been reduced, with just-in-time elevation required for admin roles. Azure DevOps OAuth based apps are now leveraging Microsoft Entra authentication with conditional access enforced. Proof-of-presence checks cover nearly all production code changes. PR.AA-01 PR.AA-02 PR.AA-03 PR.AA-04 PR.AA-05 PR.AA-06 PR.DS-01 PR.DS-02 PR.DS-10 3. Secure code deployment Code signing access is almost entirely locked to production identities. Repositories enforce branch policies with minimum approver counts. The scope of this objective has increased to optimize our secrets detection capabilities. We introduced analytics-driven validation to strengthen compliance and durability, positioning us to reduce systemic risk across engineering workflows. PR.AA-04 PR.PS-06 4. Standardize secure development pipelines Nearly all production build pipelines and 94% of release pipelines use centrally governed templates. To further improve security, we increased network isolation, which is now 89% complete, blocking direct access to public registries and flagging unvetted dependencies. Objectives Narrative CSF Sub-categories PR.DS-01 PR.DS-02 PR.DS-10 PR.IR-01 PR.PS-06 5. Protect the software supply chain Supply chain protections are maturing: Internal artifact feeds are broadly adopted and Component Governance runs by default. AI agents now remediate open-source vulnerabilities asynchronously, reducing latency between detection and resolution. These steps deliver significant gains in security posture and operational resilience, ensuring the engineering system maintains high standards across the software supply chain. GV.SC-01 GV.SC-02 GV.SC-03 GV.SC-04 GV.SC-07 GV.SC-09 ID.RA-10 PR.PS-06 Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 See appendix Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary 29 Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 30 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary 5. Monitor and detect threats We deliver comprehensive threat hunting capabilities, accelerate incident response, and help ensure consistent application of security controls by collaborating closely with engineering and security teams. We continue to drive towards completion of Objective 1. Centralized security log collection now covers more than 98% of infrastructure nodes, ensuring robust visibility and long-term retention for effective threat detection and compliance. We continue to make significant progress against Objectives 2 and 3. More than 72% of services now emit critical security logs in a standard format, and AI is being used to analyze service code and function calls to identify where security audit logs should be generated. The centralized access model for security logs has matured, with 6 out of 7 major log categories now following the minimum retention standards. Rapid anomaly detection and response capabilities have expanded through new detections and continuous validation with Red Team and Microsoft Threat Research. We have created an additional 50+ new detections to target high-priority TTPs across Microsoft infrastructure. These detections supplement the 200 we added in April, and applicable detections will be added to Microsoft Defender. This progress directly strengthens our security posture in alignment with the NIST CSF categories, specifically supporting the Identify function of Asset Management, the Protect function of Platform Security, the Detect functions of Continuous Monitoring and Anomalies and Events, and the Respond functions of Incident Mitigation and Incident Response Reporting and Communications. To align with Zero Trust principles, customers should begin by conducting a complete infrastructure inventory to identify assets, dependencies, and risk exposure. Centralizing security log collection using standardized audit formats helps ensure comprehensive visibility across services and environments, and supports consistent threat detection and compliance reporting. Robust threat intelligence is critical to enable proactive detection of anomalies, accelerate incident response, and strengthen regulatory posture. Objectives Progress 1. Complete production infrastructure inventory 2. Security log retention standards 3. Centralize access to security logs 4. Rapid anomaly detection and response 50+ new detections Customer guidance • Infrastructure inventory Use Azure Resource Graph for efficient and performant resource management Microsoft Learn • Security logging standards Define and enforce standardized logging schemas across services Azure Microsoft 365 • Threat Intelligence To enable faster threat detection, response, and operational resilience Microsoft Learn NIST CSF mapping Govern Identify Asset Management Protect Platform Security Detect Anomalies and Events Continuous Monitoring Respond Incident Mitigation Incident Response Reporting and Communications Recover See appendix Monitor and detect threats continued Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary 31 Objectives Narrative CSF Sub-categories Objectives Narrative CSF Sub-categories 1. Complete production infrastructure inventory Today, more than 98% of critical assets including network devices, physical servers, and virtual machine nodes are actively tracked through our centralized inventory system and all the security logs emitted from the nodes are centrally collected with a 2-year retention policy, ensuring telemetry is both captured and preserved to support long-term forensic analysis, audit readiness, and incident response. This visibility is essential for operational resilience, threat detection, and compliance readiness – where every asset is accounted for, monitored, and ready to support rapid investigation and remediation when needed. ID.AM-01 ID.AM-02 ID.AM-05 PR.PS-04 2. Security log retention standards More than 72% of services are now fully compliant with our standardized logging format, ensuring critical security events are captured in a uniform and actionable way. To accelerate adoption across the remaining services, we are investing in AI-driven analysis of service code repositories and function calls. This allows us to proactively identify security-sensitive operations where audit logs should be generated – closing gaps that might otherwise remain undetected. In parallel, our standard logging baseline is continuously refined. Every security incident serves as a learning opportunity, prompting updates to the baseline to reflect real-world threat scenarios and operational needs. This dynamic approach helps ensure our logging infrastructure remains resilient, responsive, and aligned with evolving security requirements. PR.PS-04 DE.CM-01 DE.CM-02 DE.CM-03 DE.CM-06 DE.CM-09 3. Centralized access to security logs To strengthen our threat detection and response capabilities, we have implemented a robust and scalable access model that enables security teams to efficiently retrieve and analyze logs for investigations and threat hunting. As of this update, the centralized access model for security logs has matured, with 6 out of 7 major log categories now following the minimum retention standards. This progress reflects deep collaboration between security and engineering teams operating in a federated model – a structure that allows telemetry gaps to be identified and resolved swiftly. PR.PS-04 DE.AE-03 4. Rapid anomaly detection and response As part of our broader strategy to continuously evaluate detection coverage, identify gaps, and stay ahead of emerging threats security and engineering collaborated to expand and refine our detection capabilities resulting in the deployment of more than 50 new detections targeting high-priority TTPs (including data exfiltration, credential access, and remote code execution). This brings our total to more than 250 active detections across production infrastructure – applicable detections will be added to Microsoft Defender. AI-driven improvements are leveraged to enhance every stage of the detection lifecycle – from identifying new detection opportunities, to accelerating development and testing, and improving signal fidelity and efficacy – helping us scale detection coverage while maintaining precision, ensuring our infrastructure remains resilient against evolving adversary techniques. DE.AE-02 DE.AE-03 DE.AE-04 DE.AE-06 DE.AE-07 RS.CO-02 RS.MI-01 RS.MI-02 See appendix Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary 32 6. Accelerate response and remediation We are increasing our ability to rapidly identify, triage, and resolve high-severity vulnerabilities to minimize risk and improve security outcomes. We continue to make progress against all objectives. When high-severity cloud vulnerabilities are discovered, we act with urgency to minimize risk. The adoption of AI-based vulnerability report triage, along with our signal intelligence (processing over 100 trillion signals everyday), has improved our ability to accurately identify and assign vulnerabilities to the correct internal teams. We expanded the program’s scope, achieving a 72% success rate addressing vulnerabilities within our reduced time to mitigate (TTM), while mitigating more vulnerabilities. Transparency remains central to the Microsoft strategy, we have published 1,096 CVEs (including 53 no-action cloud CVEs) since January 2025. Earlier this year we organized a live hacking event, called Zero Day Quest, to incentivize expert security researchers to uncover critical bugs, driving improvements across key platforms such as Azure, Identity, Microsoft 365, Dynamics, and AI. This type of event forms part of our comprehensive bounty program, which continues to set industry benchmarks – we have awarded more than USD $17 million to 344 security researchers from 59 countries, for the 12-month period of July 2024 to end of June 2025. In August we announced the return of Zero Day Quest, offering unprecedented bounty rewards of up to USD $5 million for high- impact security, reinforcing our commitment to work with the community to improve security. The Customer Security Management Office (CSMO) has improved customer engagement and incident communications, in partnership with the Microsoft Support and Security Resilience teams. Together, they have further enhanced incident response, streamlined playbooks, and improved security support. CSMO also proactively notifies customers of critical risks, ensuring CISOs receive timely updates. This progress directly strengthens our security posture in alignment with the NIST CSF categories, specifically supporting the Governance functions of Oversight and Roles, Responsibilities, and Authorities, the Identify function of Risk Assessment, the Respond functions of Analysis, Incident Management, and Incident Response Reporting and Communications, and the Recover functions of Recovery Planning and Communications. To align with Zero Trust principles, organizations should automate vulnerability detection, response, and remediation using integrated security tools and threat intelligence. Maintaining real-time visibility into security incidents across hybrid and cloud environments enables faster containment and recovery. Preparing for cloud-native incident response – including playbooks, role-based access controls, and secure logging – help ensure resilience against evolving threats. Objectives Progress 1. Accelerate vulnerability mitigation 2. Transparency of cloud vulnerabilities 53 no action cloud CVEs 3. Enhance public messaging and engagement Ongoing effort Customer guidance • Accelerate vulnerability mitigation Increase automation for vulnerability response and remediation Microsoft Learn • Prepare for cloud incident response Review best practices for incident readiness and cross-cloud response Microsoft Learn NIST CSF mapping Govern Oversight Roles, Responsibilities, and Authorities Identify Risk Assessment Protect Detect Respond Analysis Incident Management Incident Response Reporting and Communications Recover Recovery Planning Communications See appendix Accelerate response and remediation continued Objectives Narrative CSF Sub-categories Objectives Narrative CSF Sub-categories 1. Accelerate vulnerability mitigation When high-severity severity cloud vulnerabilities are discovered, we act with urgency to minimize risk. The deployment of AI-based vulnerability triage and expedited software processes, supported by signal intelligence that processes over 100 trillion signals every day, has strengthened our ability to accurately identify and assign vulnerabilities to the correct internal teams. This approach has enabled faster remediation and more efficient resolution of critical vulnerabilities, resulting in a 72% success rate, as we expanded the program’s scope and mitigated more vulnerabilities. These continued improvements have reinforced our overall security posture and helped reduce risk across cloud environments. ID.RA-01 RS.AN-03 RS.MA-02 RS.MA-03 RC.RP-01 RC.RP-02 2. Transparency of cloud vulnerabilities We continue to advance transparency in cloud vulnerability mitigation by publishing CVEs enriched with Common Weakness Enumeration™ (CWE™) and Common Platform Enumeration (CPE™) annotations. We have published 1,096 CVEs, with 53 no-action cloud CVEs, since January 2025. Internal Microsoft researchers discovered more than 48% of these vulnerabilities. The adoption of industry standards enables customers and the broader ecosystem to better understand root causes and impacted products. Investments in bounty programs and live hacking events, such as the Zero Day Quest, further demonstrate our commitment to openness and proactive security improvements across key cloud environments. ID.RA-01 RS.CO-02 RS.CO-03 3. Enhance public messaging and engagement The CSMO has improved customer engagement and incident communication by partnering with internal Microsoft Support and Security Resilience teams. By standardizing incident communications, the CSMO has increased speed and transparency in response efforts. These advances, combined with expanded executive engagement and collaboration with global security advisors, help ensure rapid, effective public messaging and proactive customer engagement during security incidents. Additionally, the CSMO proactively notifies customers of critical risks, ensuring CISOs receive timely updates. GV.OV-01 GV.OV-02 GV.OV-03 GV.RR-01 GV.RR-02 GV.RR-03 GV.RR-04 RS.CO-03 RS.CO-04 RC.CO-03 RC.CO-04 See appendix Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 Summary Culture Governance Security principles Engineering pillars Appendix < ) - - - - -- 33 Appendix: Cybersecurity frameworks in action SFI is a cybersecurity program tailored to Microsoft, focused on improving our security posture and revolutionizing the way we design, build, test, and operate our products and services, to achieve the highest security standards. This is done in part by stringently applying Zero Trust principles. To help customers understand how to map our progress with their own cybersecurity programs, we have mapped SFI progress updates to the NIST CSF and provide Zero Trust implementation guidance. The NIST CSF offers comprehensive coverage across the security landscape, enabling organizations to build cybersecurity programs that are both scalable and adaptable, the guidance is divided into the following 6 categories: Identify Protect Detect Respond Recover Govern The following image provides a high-level mapping of the SFI progress report sections to the NIST CSF Categories, more detailed information will be provided within each section of this report. NIST CSF Mapping Framework <) - ■ I ■ I ■ ■ ■ ■ I ■ ■ ■ ■ I I I ■ I I ■ I • • • • • • • • • Govern (GV) Cybersecurity Supply Chain Risk Management Engineering Organizational Context Culture Oversight Security principles P6 Policy Security principles Respond Risk Management Strategy Culture Governance Respond Roles, Responsibilities, and Authorities Culture Governance Identify (ID) Asset Management Tenants Networks Engineering Improvements Culture Security principles Risk Assessment Tenants Engineering Detect Respond Protect (PR) Awareness and Training Culture Data Security Identity Engineering Identify Management Authentication, and Access Control Identity Tenants Networks Engineering Infrastructure Resilience Culture Tenants Networks Engineering Platform Security Identity, Tenants, Networks, Engineering , Detect Detect (DE) Anomalies and Events Identity Detect Continuous Monitoring Tenants Respond (RS) Analysis Respond Incident Management Respond Incident Response Reporting and Communications Detect Respond Incident Mitigation Detect Recover (RC) Communications Respond Recovery Planning Respond It is important to note that SFI represents just one part of our broader internal commitment to NIST CSF and other regulatory frameworks. Beyond SFI, we implement additional controls, processes, and technologies that reinforce our principle of Secure by Design. This layered approach helps ensure customers benefit not only from our secure infrastructure but also from the maturity and extensibility of our security practices. CU Culture GO Governance SP Security principles P1 Identity P2 Tenants P3 Networks P4 Engineering P5 Detect P6 Respond Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary 34 SP P6 P6 NIST CSF category descriptions This section explains the NIST CSF categories used in mapping to SFI. Each NIST CSF function contains several categories, and each category contains at least one sub-category. To navigate these, we follow the standard naming format: function.category-sub-category Example: The Governance (GV) function has a category of Organizational Context (OC), and the first sub-category (01) states “The organizational mission is understood and informs cybersecurity risk management”. This would be written as GV.OC-01 Govern (GV) Cybersecurity Supply Chain Risk Management (GV.SC) Cyber supply chain risk management processes are identified, established, managed, monitored, and improved by organizational stakeholders. Organizational Context (GV.OC) The circumstances – mission, stakeholder expectations, dependencies, and legal, regulatory, and contractual requirements – surrounding the organization’s cybersecurity risk management decisions are understood. Oversight (GV.OV) Results of organization-wide cybersecurity risk management activities and performance are used to inform, improve, and adjust the risk management strategy. Policy (GV.PO) Organizational cybersecurity policy is established, communicated, and enforced. Risk Management Strategy (GV.RM) The organization’s priorities, constraints, risk tolerance and appetite statements, and assumptions are established, communicated, and used to support operational risk decisions. Roles, Responsibilities, and Authorities (GV.RR) Cybersecurity roles, responsibilities, and authorities to foster accountability, performance assessment, and continuous improvement are established and communicated. Identify (ID) Asset Management (ID.AM) Assets (e.g., data, hardware, software, systems, facilities, services, people) that enable the organization to achieve business purposes are identified and managed consistent with their relative importance to organizational objectives and the organization’s risk strategy. Improvements (ID.IM) Improvements to organizational cybersecurity risk management processes, procedures and activities are identified across all CSF Functions. Risk Assessment (ID.RA) The cybersecurity risk to the organization, assets, and individuals is understood by the organization. Protect (PR) Awareness and Training (PR.AT) The organization’s personnel are provided with cybersecurity awareness and training so that they can perform their cybersecurity-related tasks. Data Security (PR.IR) Data are managed consistent with the organization’s risk strategy to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information. Identify Management Authentication, and Access Control (PR.AA) Access to physical and logical assets is limited to authorized users, services, and hardware and managed commensurate with the assessed risk of unauthorized access. Infrastructure Resilience (PR.IR) Security architectures are managed with the organization’s risk strategy to protect asset confidentiality, integrity, and availability, and organizational resilience. Platform Security (PR.PS) The hardware, software (e.g., firmware, operating systems, applications), and services of physical and virtual platforms are managed consistent with the organization’s risk strategy to protect their confidentiality, integrity, and availability. Detect (DE) Anomalies and Events (DE.AE) Assets are monitored to find anomalies, indicators of compromise, and other potentially adverse events. Continuous Monitoring (DE.CM) Anomalies, indicators of compromise, and other potentially adverse events are analyzed to characterize the events and detect cybersecurity incidents. Respond (RS) Analysis (RS.AN) Investigations are conducted to ensure effective response and support forensics and recovery activities. Incident Management (RS.MA) Responses to detected cybersecurity incidents are managed. Incident Mitigation (RS.MI) Activities are performed to prevent expansion of an event and mitigate its effects. Incident Response Reporting and Communications (RS.CO) Response activities are coordinated with internal and external stakeholders as required by laws, regulations, or policies. Recover (RC) Communications (RS.CO) Restoration activities are coordinated with internal and external parties.. Recovery Planning (RC.RP) Restoration activities are performed to ensure operational availability of systems and services affected by cybersecurity incidents. Microsoft SFI Progress Report: November 2025 Engineering pillars Appendix Culture Security principles Governance Summary < ) 35 Culture, Governance, and Security principles NIST CSF mapping Culture Focus area CSF categories CSF Sub-categories Security is the the number 1 priority for Microsoft Govern Policy Roles, Responsibilities, and Authorities Identif