le time to better appreciate how a change of perspective a ect perception and in uence trainee own experience of the situation each of the four scenario follows this design and always o er the trainee to option to experience the situation from one perspective after the other the second key design decision regarding the training session wa to leave the discussion about what can be done about a given situation to a live discussion between trainee and facilitator in stead of building it into the vr application this is why the training is meant to be performed in small group of at most trainee in the presence of a facilitator in person training is also useful a many trainee are not familiar with vr headset and need help getting started see section we sought to make the training a immersive and realistic a possible first each scenario is based on real incident that were informed and synthesized from experience had or witnessed by the design group second we ensured that the actor shared identity characteristic with the protagonist so their voice and dialog would sound real third the location e g meeting room and o ce space a showcased in gure and in simulation apps e g video call system are based on real corporate o ce and product e g google meet so the environment is a believable and a familiar a possible to avoid the suspension of disbelief e ect scenario development the four scenario used during the training were developed be tween and by a group of google volunteer involved in google dei activity in partnership with a vr studio each scenario wa developed iteratively following the three phase pro cess summarized below due to the sensitive nature of the topic discussed the design session were not recorded initial framing for behavior change the initial design phase fo cuses on selecting which behavior the training aimed at changing participating googlers were prompted to nd which behavior to target by answering the following question in a group setting what are the key 