o not have detailed information on the action participant took in the vr system e g which scenario they completed a which character a we decided to not perform any logging to increase participant freedom in exploring the scenario chi may honolulu hawaii elie bursztein karla brown patrick gage kelley and leonie sanderson scale number of response q overall on a scale of not satisfied to very satisfied how satisfied are you with the vr training scale number of response q overall on a scale of not useful to very useful how useful do you feel the training wa at making you better identify unconscious bias and how to avoid scale number of response q on a scale of significantly worse to significantly better how would you rate the vr training experience compared to our dei traditional training figure post training survey histogram for three question overall satisfaction with the training usefulness towards iden tifying unconscious bias and preference for vr compared to traditional dei training positive aspect the open ended survey response show that participant thought our vr training improved upon traditional training in two key way increased understanding participant felt that the training made the issue more real and helped them understand the issue from the multiple other perspective than their own r microaggressions felt more real r opportunity to experience microaggressions from the perspective of a member of a historically underrepresented group r it wa immer sive and attention grabbing in a way that previous training have not been r the change of point of view wa a good and realistic experience enabling seeing the same scene from di erent point of view physically which help to tune the mind to the di erent personal experience r made em pathy more real via experience participant also noted that being an active player in the situation instead of a passive reader or listener helped with realism r role change were great instead of a passive participant it felt more real enjoyment participant reported that vr training wa more enjoyable than standard training and that it wa easier to focus r the time just ew by r immersion remove the sense of time from the training you are focused r it wa easier to keep attention to the scene due to the immersion it looked shorter and less boring to go through the training content vr usability echoing the result of our testing phase section multiple participant reported a lack of familiarity and discomfort with using a virtual reality headset r i did not like the vr setup but loved the p view of the case a lot more r vr itself in dependent of training remained uncomfortable also echoing our initial tester participant also reported quest usability issue for people with glass r not too glass people friendly even with the spacer area of improvement participant feedback on what could be improved fell into two broad category realism some participant expressed that the overall graphic quality wa not good enough and degraded their training experience r part of the training where a lot of head motion and low resolution element e g people on video were very disorienting they also expressed that the interface wa laggy and scenario had loading issue r some delay in loading forest scenario this corroborates our test user feedback who also wanted improved realism which they too felt wa critical for a truly immersive experience section lack of agency depth of scenario this wa the most re ported limitation with participant wanting more avenue to in uence the outcome of the scenario for instance r expressed the wish to pick answer in uence the outcome of the conversation and r wa interested in experimenting on what happens if an individual push back on being called out for a microaggression discussion and future work from this initial investigation conducting dei training within virtual reality is reported to create a more e ective and immersive experience through simulated direct interaction with scenario instead of traditional reading or listening participant are more engaged and empathetic this open up a path to complement or even replace standard dei training with a form of training that may feel less like a chore and more like a truly meaningful experience however using vr is currently hampered with usability and quality concern we summarize ve concern that exist today a hurdle to vr adoption hardware issue several of our tester and training partici pant experienced issue with the vr headset themselves most often discomfort due to weight or glass though other work ha found some experience dizziness and current mod el are also inaccessible to some user some of these issue will be addressed with improvement over time from vr hardware manufacturer but alternative medium for train ing will need to continue to be o ered scaling cost scaling vr training to a large workforce is challenging due to hardware cost scaling tech support re mote access to technology and training enough facilitator one option to facilitate scaling is to move the debrief s sion to the virtual world which a we discussed earlier may improve participation section and could also allow ac cess by people in multiple o ce or even by remote worker while this would also require wider distribution of headset we see this a a promising line of future exploration and testing fidelity quality while the current training show promis ing result it continues to fall short in term of delity mak ing the training more realistic requires a state of the art game engine the design of high delity environment and leveraging virtual reality to enhance dei training at google avatar that are able to portray more realistic body language a wider range of facial expression and higher quality char acter design unfortunately these improvement all have trade o s shifting to higher delity environment and character drastically increase production cost and may restrict the training to only the highest end and also most expensive headset scenario depth participant wanted more agency more free dom of movement and engagement more complex storyline with more conversational turn and more option for po tential action this is the most di cult area to improve upon a it requires multi branching script where partic ipants can react in more way alternatively very recent advance in ai open the door to create unscripted gen erative interaction with character however using such technology for training purpose is unproven and would require further research and strong guardrail to meet learning objective and not lead to unexpected or o ensive response in sensitive conversation we plan to prototype such character in future work to evaluate the feasibility of having them meaningfully respond to trainee with more nuanced body language vocal tone and facial expression novelty finally a variable we couldn t control for is how much this positive feedback is due to the novelty e ect ver sus the vr training being an intrinsically more immersive training experience while novelty concern may subside due to more common normalized use of vr in many a pects of life this may also reduce the engagement our initial testing and training participant reported in this case study while these concern cannot be understated we believe there are way each can be mitigated through further development testing and research we have shown that shifting dei training into vr show promise a a way to reinvigorate these session which should lead to more inclusive safe workplace keeping that ultimate goal in mind inspires u to continue investing in understanding and improving vr dei training,5407,4,"[1.44914485e-308 8.14408165e-309 9.32248224e-309 9.79077235e-309
