and industry procurement roadblock are often one of the most significant challenge that government and industry face when it come to adopting new technology like ai to clear these barrier government should adopt cloud first standard to promote the uptake of ai and adopt transparent procurement rule that encourage fair competition finally government will need more ai expertise to effectively harness ai policymakers should build and scale up in house ai skilling for the government it workforce google took a similar step a few year ago requiring all software engineer to enroll in an internal machine learning curriculum government should also consider creative way to bring in private sector talent such a ai fellow modeled on the u s presidential innovation fellow program and the uk s government digital service finally while every agency will need some ai expertise government should consider establishing a centralized resource of expert that can advise agency across the government helping traditional industry and small business use ai small business and traditional industry have too often lagged behind their peer in adoption of innovative technology policymakers and ai developer must work together to develop aggressive outreach strategy to traditional industry and small business who have much to gain from ai adoption in term of their competitive posture if they are quick to harness and deploy ai for example a recent study showed that of small business using ai had success in driving revenue customer outreach and acquisition or increasing productivity however adopting ai may not be the first priority for harried small business owner or industry that are accustomed to taking a wait and see approach to new technology to address this gap policymakers in all country should create a national strategy to spur ai adoption across industry for example in the u s building on recommendation from the national artificial intelligence advisory committee to create a multi agency task force to develop framework for small and medium sized organization to adopt trustworthy ai identify priority national sector that have the highest need and or the lowest uptake of ai tool such a the agriculture manufacturing health care and energy sector and work with these sector on proof of concept initiative to model effective ai deployment promoting widespread adoption and universal accessibility give small business a digital jumpstart through new model of technical assistance and engagement including digital coach who can help business understand and leverage ai to capitalize on new opportunity target ai training resource towards small business and traditional industry in underserved community including through program like the small business ai innovation and commercialization institute contemplated in the recent u executive order improve access to capital including by introducing low interest loan and grant program designed to support ai driven transformation enabling regulation at the same time government need to ensure that their regulatory framework empower and don t frustrate small business and traditional industry seeking to adopt ai without pro innovation legal framework program to promote adoption of ai will be running uphill regulator should not only ask how they can address risk proportionately they should consider what regulation will facilitate the adoption of ai including adoption by small business with fewer resource to ensure that ai is accessible to all government should adopt a proportionate risk based framework focused on application recognizing that ai is a multi purpose technology and regulatory requirement should be calibrated to the particular risk and use case ensure parity in expectation between non ai and ai system recognizing that even imperfect ai system can improve on existing process promote use of technical standard so that where a business is required to show it compliance with a regulation it can do so by showing adherence to a co