thering and problem solving would help worker avoid becoming overreliant on ai limitation our study ha limitation that warrant consideration and o er avenue for future research firstly we observed that participant occasionally con ated reduced e ort in using genai with reduced e ort in critical thinking with genai this misconception may stem from the infrequent contemplation of critical thinking in their daily task regardless of whether they use genai potentially leading to inaccurate self reporting this con ation often occurred when participant were satis ed with ai generated response suggesting that when ai produce expected outcome user may engage in less critical evaluation future study could employ alternative measure of critical thinking such a think aloud protocol or task based assessment to better di erentiate between e ort reduction and critical thinking process secondly we assess user subjective task con dence following prior work on ai assisted decision making still one s subjective self con dence may not always be well calibrated with respect to objective expertise on task future work should explore this subjective objective distinction in the context of critical thinking with genai in knowledge work thirdly our survey wa conducted exclusively in english with participant required to be uent english speaker this approach ensured consistency in data collection and feasibility of analysis by our english speaking research team but ha no representation of non english speaking population or multilingual context future research could explore cross linguistic and cross cultural perspec tives on genai usage and critical thinking fourthly our sample wa biased towards younger more tech nologically skilled participant who regularly use genai tool at work at least once per week this demographic skew may not fully represent the broader population of knowledge worker poten tially overlooking the experience and perception of older or less tech oriented professional lastl