like those in business and nancial operation may delegate task to ai and focus more on the part of their job that require a human touch understanding which occupation have task within the current frontier of ai capability is important because these job are most likely to see productivity boost or a shift in their core task in addition understanding the current frontier of ai applicability allows system designer to investigate where ai is currently falling short and see if it is due to an addressable technical shortcoming such a low satisfaction for data analysis a mismatch in modality such a moving object or a societal factor such a law or regulation looking ahead a ai system gain functionality and adoption continues to evolve we can use our ai applicability score to see how the ai frontier shift over time our result show that ai is most applicable to information work occupation and often play the role of an advisor or teacher providing expertise to the user in the same way that word processor turned typing into a widespread skill rather than a core task for typist and secretary ai too may democratize skill around information work to the extent that people can successfully apply ai expertise shrinking the performance gap between low and high skilled worker this provides some empirical backing to autor s theoretical argument that ai could reduce income inequality if ai can broaden access to expertise and people have su cient foundational knowledge to apply and evaluate ai assistance then ai could provide more worker access to previously niche expert work potentially shrinking economic inequality our user goal versus ai action distinction relates to a key question in the literature and public discourse around ai to what extent is ai automating versus augmenting work activity it is tempting to conclude that occupation that have high ai action applicability score will be automated and thus experience job or wage loss and that occupation with high user goal applicability score will be augmented and raise wage this would be a mistake a downstream consequence of new technology are very hard to predict and often counterintuitive take the example of atm which automated a core task of bank teller but led to an increase in the number of bank teller job a bank opened more branch at lower cost and teller focused on more valuable relationship building rather than processing deposit and withdrawal our measure of ai applicability indicate where ai is likely to change work which is a part of the automation v augmentation question but ai s e ect on employment and wage will depend on hard to predict business decision there are some natural limitation to the conclusion that can be drawn from our data our task completion and scope metric are measure of ai s utility towards an iwa but they do no capture the productivity impact on people doing that iwa another gap is the di erence between the way work activity are performed in occupation compared to in our data for instance provide general assistance mean something di erent for a passenger attendant and for copilot our data also represents only one slice of the ai market there are many other ai platform including more task or occupation speci c llm which are not represented in our data while decomposing an occupation into it work activity is standard practice in the literature it doe not provide a complete representation of every occupation the connecting glue between task also contributes to the value of work finally our use of o net mean our result are shaped by it u s centric view may lag behind current workplace activity and do not capture valuable task performed outside of occupation e g work in the home or volunteering modernizing our understanding of workplace activity will be crucial a generative ai continues to change how work is done this work give rise to a number of future research question of high importance to society we measured how ai capability overlap with work activity but it remains to be seen how di erent occupation refactor their work responsibility in response to ai s rapid progress in addition entirely new occupation may emerge performing new type of work activity this is not a new phenomenon the majority of employment today is in occupation that arose in the last year a a result of new technology which new job emerge and how old one are reconstituted is a crucial future research direction information work ha become central to the global economy embedded in the majority of occupation technological advance of the last several decade such a the computer the internet and the search engine have provided valuable tool for storing processing and nding information more e ciently our result highlight how llm can contribute to broader part of the information life cycle including creation inter pretation and communication in more exible way than earlier technology a generative ai continues to progress it will be important to map the frontier of ai functionality to understand what additional capability ai is providing people and how that new functionality can be applied in the workplace material and method bing copilot data we analyze anonymized and privacy scrubbed u s conversation data from microsoft bing copilot hence forth copilot gathered from january to september we focus only on conversation in the united state to align with occupation and work activity information from o net our main dataset is a uniform sample of approximately k conversation providing a representative view of what task user perform with a mainstream publicly available free to use generative ai chatbot we use a supporting dataset of k conversation uniformly sampled from those that received at least one thumb up or thumb down reaction from the user this dataset allows u to investigate what activity are performed more or less successfully a measured by explicit user feedback our use of copilot data wa reviewed and approved by the microsoft irb under id while we cannot releas