Level_2_Meso,Microsoft,AI-and-Reading-Study.pdf,"AI and Traditional Learning: Complementary Strategies for Deeper Learning Understanding how the use of generative AI impacts learning outcomes is key to creating effective guidelines for AI in education. Microsoft Research (MSR) and Cambridge University Press & Assessment conducted one of the first large-scale quantitative investigations into the impact of large language models (LLMs) on the foundations of learning: understanding and retaining information. The research measured students’ comprehension and retention of two texts. Participants were randomly assigned to study each text using one of three strategies: traditional note-taking, using an AI chatbot to answer questions about the text, or a combination of both. The findings reveal the continued importance of traditional learning approaches. They also demonstrate the benefits of combining AI use with traditional learning over using AI alone, and they point to the AI skills that students need to maximize those benefits. Methodology • 405 students aged 14–15 in UK secondary schools studied two text passages. Their comprehension and retention were assessed three days later. • Group 1 studied one of the text passages using AI only and the other passage by taking notes. Group 2 used AI only on one text passage and a combination of AI and note-taking for the other passage. • The study also explored students’ learning experiences when using the different strategies, including which they preferred and why. • Preliminary research findings: Effects of generative AI and note-taking on reading comprehension and memory: A randomised experiment in secondary schools • Read more about the latest AI in Education insights. AI and Traditional Learning Two Sides of the Story: Effectiveness vs. Enjoyment Effectiveness Note-taking—whether done alone or alongside AI use—produced higher comprehension and retention scores compared to using AI alone, underscoring the importance and effectiveness of traditional active learning strategies. Enjoyment At the same time, students generally used AI constructively and perceived it as more enjoyable, more helpful, and preferable to note-taking. Students preferred AI for its ability to answer questions, simplify complex material, and provide immediate feedback. Group 1: AI vs. Notes Group 2: AI vs. AI + Notes AI AI Notes AI + Notes 100% 75% 25% 50% 0% Test performance The Takeaway: • AI should be positioned as a complement—not a substitute— for established practices like note-taking, which remain essential for learning. • By integrating AI thoughtfully with traditional methods, educators may achieve enhanced learning outcomes that neither approach can deliver alone. • Leveraging input from educators and researchers in the design and use of new AI-based tools for learning is key to realizing these benefits. AI and Traditional Learning AI Provides New and Unique Learning Opportunities The vast majority of students’ AI use was constructive— seeking additional information and deeper understanding— rather than distracting or reductive. Seeking additional information and deeper understanding Understanding the text Interacting with the bot Information condensation Irrelevant, off-topic, miscellaneous Study and memory help Prompt type used Percent of students Most often At least once 100% 0% 50% 25% 75% Most students demonstrated remarkable curiosity, asking sophisticated questions that extended beyond the immediate text. These explorations represent a different kind of active learning opportunity that may not result from note-taking alone, underscoring AI’s potential to expand intellectual horizons. To foster this behavior, educators can: • Actively encourage students to use AI productively, such as asking targeted questions that help them clarify specific misunderstandings, engage in critical thinking, and integrate information. • Discourage students from using LLMs for passive consumption of automatic summaries and explanations. • Leverage insights from students’ interactions with AI to better understand what concepts they are struggling with or what they are curious about, and tailor future lessons and activities accordingly.",ai and traditional learning complementary strategy for deeper learning understanding how the use of generative ai impact learning outcome is key to creating effective guideline for ai in education microsoft research msr and cambridge university press assessment conducted one of the first large scale quantitative investigation into the impact of large language model llm on the foundation of learning understanding and retaining information the research measured student comprehension and retention of two text participant were randomly assigned to study each text using one of three strategy traditional note taking using an ai chatbot to answer question about the text or a combination of both the finding reveal the continued importance of traditional learning approach they also demonstrate the benefit of combining ai use with traditional learning over using ai alone and they point to the ai skill that student need to maximize those benefit methodology student aged in uk secondary school studied two text passage their comprehension and retention were assessed three day later group studied one of the text passage using ai only and the other passage by taking note group used ai only on one text passage and a combination of ai and note taking for the other passage the study also explored student learning experience when using the different strategy including which they preferred and why preliminary research finding effect of generative ai and note taking on reading comprehension and memory a randomised experiment in secondary school read more about the latest ai in education insight ai and traditional learning two side of the story effectiveness v enjoyment effectiveness note taking whether done alone or alongside ai use produced higher comprehension and retention score compared to using ai alone underscoring the importance and effectiveness of traditional active learning strategy enjoyment at the same time student generally used ai constructively and perceived it a more enjoyable more helpful and preferable to note taking student preferred ai for it ability to answer question simplify complex material and provide immediate feedback group ai v note group ai v ai note ai ai note ai note test performance the takeaway ai should be positioned a a complement not a substitute for established practice like note taking which remain essential for learning by integrating ai thoughtfully with traditional method educator may achieve enhanced learning outcome that neither approach can deliver alone leveraging input from educator and researcher in the design and use of new ai based tool for learning is key to realizing these benefit ai and traditional learning ai provides new and unique learning opportunity the vast majority of student ai use wa constructive seeking additional information and deeper understanding rather than distracting or reductive seeking additional information and deeper understanding understanding the text interacting with the bot information condensation irrelevant off topic miscellaneous study and memory help prompt type used percent of student most often at least once most student demonstrated remarkable curiosity asking sophisticated question that extended beyond the immediate text these exploration represent a different kind of active learning opportunity that may not result from note taking alone underscoring ai s potential to expand intellectual horizon to foster this behavior educator can actively encourage student to use ai productively such a asking targeted question that help them clarify specific misunderstanding engage in critical thinking and integrate information discourage student from using llm for passive consumption of automatic summary and explanation leverage insight from student interaction 