gh self reported productivity when using llm based tool with perceived time saved substantially exceeding actual time saved finally although not the focus of this phase of the research we observed early evidence that there are task for which the productivity gain are more complex and may be harder to actualize in certain case with current llm based tool providing a new set of option rather than simply accelerating existing way of working below we first provide a brief description of the study whose result are included in this first research report we then present cross cutting finding of the study focusing on three dimension of productivity speed quality and effort finally we close with discussion and future work study description in this section we provide a brief overview of the study included in this first report many are or will be supported by separate dedicated manuscript and we leave methodological detail to those manuscript providing link where possible while none of these study have yet gone through peer review several are currently under review with more to be submitted soon note also that in addition to the eight study presented here the ai and productivity research team ha many others in progress these will be discussed in future report a their finding develop when we discus result of the study that involve comparison e g between a treatment and control we only report result that were determined to be significant by a traditional statistical test unless otherwise noted copilot common task study this experiment assessed productivity gain from copilot across multiple task common among office worker email information retrieval intranet sharepoint information retrieval content creation and meeting summarization we recruited participant via the upwork platform we randomly divided participant into treatment copilot and control no copilot group to assess the quality of participant information retrieval we asked them question about the information they found and