ation for the time being and the labor market implication of increasingly capable ai could be similarly affected by such force for example gans and goldfarb argue that the presence of bottleneck task within job mean that partial ai automation can lead to an increase in labor income a such task increase in economic value at least until a job is entirely automated conclusion the upshot of this chapter is that the impact of ai on the economy is unlikely to be uniform a our effective ai coverage framework illustrates the labor market implication for different worker will hinge on how reliable frontier ai tool are for their most central task but the labor market effect may also depend on the skill requirement of task that ai can proficiently handle relative to the rest of the economy indeed we find that removing task claude can already handle from the economy would produce a net deskilling effect the task remaining for human have lower educational requirement than those handled by ai while highly suggestive this may miss an important detail the most complex task where claude is used tend also to be those where it struggle most rather than displacing highly skilled professional this could instead reinforce the value of their complementary expertise in understanding ai s work and assessing it quality the anthropic economic index report the counterpart to these transformative labor market effect is the broader impact on growth and productivity on the one hand incorporating task reliability into our analysis diminishes the implied effect on labor productivity growth a informed by current claude usage pattern if bottleneck task bind the implied impact diminishes further on the other hand the continuing growth in model capability suggests that both task coverage and task success may increase which in turn could increase productivity impact when we study the correlation between primitive with the o net we restrict to task appearing in at least conversation to reduce measurement