t or trade with china the effect may not be immediately clear from aggregate unemployment data factor like trade policy and the business cycle could cloud interpretation of trendlines labor market impact of ai one common approach is to compare outcome between more or less ai exposed worker firm or industry in order to isolate the effect of ai from confounding force exposure is typically defined at the task level ai can grade homework but not manage a classroom for example so teacher are considered less exposed than worker whose entire job can be performed remotely our work follows this task based approach incorporating measure of theoretical ai capability and real world usage before aggregating to occupation measuring exposure our approach combine data from three source the o net database which enumerates task associated with around unique occupation in the u our own usage data a measured in the anthropic economic index task level exposure estimate from eloundou et al which measure whether it is theoretically possible for an llm to make a task at least twice a fast figure share of claude usage by eloundou et al task exposure rating this figure show claude usage distributed across o net task grouped by their theoretical ai exposure task rated fully feasible for an llm alone account for of observed claude usage while task rated not feasible account for just data on claude usage come from the previous four economic index report labor market impact of ai eloundou et al s metric score task on a simple scale if a task can be doubled in speed by an llm alone if it requires additional tool or software built on top of the llm and otherwise why might actual usage fall short of theoretical capability some task that are theoretically possible may not show up in usage because of model limitation others may be slow to diffuse due to legal constraint specific software requirement human verification step or other hurdle for example eloundou et al mark authorize drug refill and prov