e for worker older than this may provide some signal of the early effect of ai on employment and echo the finding from brynjolfsson et al but there are several alternative interpretation the young worker who are not hired may be remaining at their existing job taking different job or returning to school a further data related caveat is that job transition may be more vulnerable to mismeasurement in survey discussion this report introduces a new measure for understanding the labor market effect of ai and study impact on unemployment and hiring job are more exposed to ai to the extent that their task are theoretically feasible figure new job start among worker age in occupation with high observed exposure and no ai exposure current population survey the top panel show the percent of young worker starting new job in high v no exposure occupation the bottom panel measure the gap between these two series in a difference in difference framework labor market impact of ai with llm and observed on our platform in automated work related use case we find that computer programmer customer service representative and financial analyst are among the most exposed using survey data from the u we find no impact on unemployment rate for worker in the most exposed occupation although there s tentative evidence that hiring into those profession ha slowed slightly for worker aged our work is a first step toward cataloging the impact of ai on the labor market we hope that the analytical step taken in this report especially around coverage and counterfactuals will be easy to update a new data on employment and ai usage emerge an established approach may help future observer separate signal from noise there are several improvement to be made to the present work our usage data will be incorporated in future update forming an evolving picture of task and job coverage in the economy the eloundou et al metric could also be updated to the extent that it is linked to llm capability a of early and